WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.840
Gripping the mouse with your thumb,&nbsp;
ring and pinky, over the edge of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.720
mouse allows for unparalleled stopping&nbsp;
power and aiming speed. This is extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.600
useful for flashy target switching, tracking&nbsp;
erratic targets and small aiming adjustments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.400
Let’s take a look at how to correctly use this&nbsp;
technique, and why it can be so effective.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.000
Okay, so you will need to be using a mouse&nbsp;
grip where your fingertips are pressing into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.720
your mouse, check out our optimal mouse grip&nbsp;
video in the description for more info on that.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.240
If available to you, set the lift off&nbsp;
distance of your mouse to the highest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.840
setting. You don’t want your mouse&nbsp;
missing input when using this technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.480
Depending on the mouse, the degree to&nbsp;
which you can use this technique will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.720
change. For example on the G303 Shroud edition&nbsp;
you can’t configure the lift off distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.160
so I can only prop up the mouse a&nbsp;
small amount before it stops working.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.680
Keeping in mind that more isn’t necessarily better&nbsp;
but it is nice to have the freedom to find out.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.640
You will also want to keep your nails trimmed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.920
if they get too long they’ll get in the&nbsp;
way of your fingertips and the mousepad.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.440
Alright, so what makes this&nbsp;
technique so effective?

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.560
Well, Moving the mouse around on your fingertips&nbsp;
means you can dig them in whenever you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.920
instantly stop. To clarify that point, it’s&nbsp;
less about digging them directly down into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.800
mousepad, but moreso about tucking them further&nbsp;
under the mouse which really puts the breaks on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.280
You can see here we are moving the mouse&nbsp;
with the fingers in their normal position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.040
and it glides just fine, but as&nbsp;
soon as we wedge the fingers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.840
the mouse hits a wall and&nbsp;
comes to a complete stop.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.720
Without this technique, your stopping&nbsp;
power during a flick or target acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.360
relies heavily on the muscles currently moving&nbsp;
the mouse to suddenly stop what they’re doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.080
and then apply the right amount of force in&nbsp;
the opposite direction to come to a quick stop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.520
If that movement in the opposite direction is&nbsp;
even slightly too strong where it bounces back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.680
or too weak where you overshoot the mark, your&nbsp;
aim can be thrown off from where you intended.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.000
Generally speaking, stopping power and speed&nbsp;
are typically considered to be tradeoffs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.440
to have one, you can’t really have the other.&nbsp;
This is why higher friction, slower mousepads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.760
are usually associated with flicking aim&nbsp;
because it grants you more stopping power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.280
while low friction fast mousepads are associated&nbsp;
with tracking aim due to reduced static friction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.240
that makes adjustments and moving&nbsp;
the mouse at different speeds easier.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.800
This mouse grip technique lets&nbsp;
you get the best of both worlds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.880
You get the incredible stopping power of&nbsp;
your fingertips, while also being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.880
choose your speed by adjusting the pressure&nbsp;
of your fingertips against the mousepad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.120
Pressing down will slow things down,&nbsp;
while pulling up will speed things up.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.520
For a practical example, pulling up to increase&nbsp;
speed can be great for close range tracking where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:57.120
an enemy is moving very quickly and erratically.&nbsp;
This lets you make adjustments faster to keep on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.160
target. At mid-range you may want to apply&nbsp;
more pressure to slow things down for more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.680
control so you don’t overaim and miss the mark.&nbsp;
Aiming at long ranges while still maintaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.600
headshot level precision can be achieved&nbsp;
by applying a great amount of pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.560
such that your fingertips anchor against the&nbsp;
pad. From this position you can essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.000
push the mouse against these walls to achieve&nbsp;
extremely precise aim within this range of motion.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.160
And there we have it, an advanced&nbsp;
mouse grip technique that puts you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.760
in control of the variables, and&nbsp;
gives you an answer for any situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.480
It will take some time to find the&nbsp;
exact grip that performs best for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.120
and to learn how to control it. For a quick&nbsp;
look at how it feels, open up KovaaK’s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.080
play some Tile Frenzy with this grip to see&nbsp;
just how much faster you can aim with it. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:49.200
basic and intermediate playlists are also a great&nbsp;
place to experiment with and learn the technique.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.240
We hope you found this video helpful, let&nbsp;
us know how you go in the comments below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.840
subscribe for more content like this,&nbsp;
and we’ll catch you in the next one.

